<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigismund I the Old</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1467">1467</a>–<a href="/years/1548">1548</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sigismund I of Poland · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigismund I the Old was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and grand duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1506 to 1548. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-krakow">Treaty of Kraków</a> in 1525 he accepted the homage of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>, who secularised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s lands as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> under Polish overlordship. His reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> flourish at the royal court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old.jpg" alt="Sigismund I the Old" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans von Kulmbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kulmbach_Sigismund_I_the_Old.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sigismund was born on 1 January 1467 at Kozienice, the fifth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a>, and was not expected to rule. When Casimir died in 1492 he was the only son without titles or land. His efforts to get land from his brother Alexander and an Austrian throne failed, as did an invasion of Moldavia in 1497. His eldest brother Vladislaus II, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, then granted him the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> in 1499 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> in 1501, and in 1504 he became governor of Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Alexander I died in 1506, the Lithuanian Ducal Council elected Sigismund grand duke on 20 September, and the Polish Senate at Piotrków elected him king on 8 December. He was crowned at Wawel Cathedral on 24 January 1507. He summoned annual sessions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> and issued statutes between 1530 and 1538 that fixed the free election of the monarch. His reforms included a permanent conscription army in 1527 and the recovery of royal properties that had been pawned to nobles, which provoked the Chicken War of 1537, a revolt of the lesser nobility at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>. Sigismund rejected most of the demands and accepted only the principle that one person could not hold two offices.</p><p>War against Muscovy brought the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in 1514 but a Polish and Lithuanian victory at Orsha on 8 September 1514, and the commander Jan Amor Tarnowski stopped a Muscovite invasion at Starodub in 1535. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-krakow">Treaty of Kraków</a> of 1525 ended two centuries of wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>: the Order was secularised and his nephew Albert, who had become a Lutheran, became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and paid homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-iii-of-masovia">Janusz III of Masovia</a> in 1526 Sigismund united that duchy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> with the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sigismund married first Barbara Zápolya and then Bona Sforza, daughter of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, who supported his purchase of land and improvements of the royal estates. Under her influence Italian styles dominated at the height of the Polish Renaissance. His only surviving legitimate son, Sigismund Augustus, was made co-king in 1529 and took the throne when his father died on 1 April 1548.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sigismund I the Old” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 10 April 1525 Albert resigned his office, became a Protestant and, in the Prussian Homage in Kraków, was granted the title Duke of Prussia by his uncle, King <b>Sigismund I of Poland</b>, under the Treaty of Kraków.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Piast lines died out and disputes followed, Głogów was granted to the Jagiellonian <b>Sigismund</b>, who ruled it from 1499 until 1506 before becoming King of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glogow">Duchy of Głogów</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kozienice, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.59,21.55&amp;year=1519" aria-label="Kozienice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5037 378 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sigismund I the Old"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5487 648h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5487 648h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.59,21.55&amp;year=1519">What was here in 1519? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
